From d50f81ed00fa9c0a252ef0c2658e518c885dcc06 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: lucascouts <lucascs@protonmail.com>
Date: Fri, 27 Nov 2026 20:25:00 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] agent_sandbox, agent_ui, agent_servers: fixes from the
 2026-11-27 review of 0.31.0

Findings Z1-Z10 of hub docs/assets/security-review-0.31.0-2026-11-27/README.md, fixed here
but for Z4 (the release scripts, outside the series). Decisions are the operator's, same day.

Z1, Z2 -- the login of a jailed agent. The login task came from the auth method the agent
advertises in `initialize`, command, args and env included, and a jailed agent answers that
from inside the jail; Zeo ran it on the host, outside the jail, on the operator's click. It
also ran with CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR on the jail's own config dir, which the agent writes: hooks,
a statusLine or an apiKeyHelper in settings.json, an MCP server in .claude.json, a plugin
would all run on the host. Now a jailed login runs the agent program Zeo resolved for the
launch (`LaunchRecord::login_program`) with fixed arguments for Plus's three methods, any
other method is refused, nothing of the agent's environment or cwd is kept, and it runs
against an empty private directory (`DirBuilder::create`, 0700, removed afterwards); only
`.credentials.json` is then moved into the jail's directory -- a new file, `create_new`, then
a rename, after checking the directory is a real one of this user, so a link the agent
planted is replaced, not written through. The Agent Sandbox page no longer tells the
operator to run `CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR=<state>/claude claude` by hand (the same hazard); its
string is gone from all nine catalogs.

Z3 -- the state root. Its default was /var/tmp/zeo-jail, where every user can create a
project's predictable folder first and receive the login and the copied transcripts. The
default is now ~/.local/state/zeo/jail, and every folder from the state root down to the
project's claude/ must be a real directory of this user, the ones below the root mode 0700,
or the start is refused. A jail's state from before moves with nothing: a new login is due.

Z5, Z6 -- the ssh-agent. Nothing could tell the operator's dedicated agent from the
desktop's /tmp/ssh-XXXX/agent.N, whose keys sign unconfirmed; the kill switch passed the
setting raw (`~` never expanded) and logged `ssh_agent_emptied` when ssh-add started, not
when it succeeded. Operator's decision: Zeo starts the jails' own agent. New setting
`agent_sandbox.ssh_agent` (default false): Zeo makes /tmp/zeo-ssh-$UID (0700, a folder made
first by someone else, or open to others, is refused), starts `ssh-agent -a` there when
nothing answers, and the operator loads it with `SSH_AUTH_SOCK=... ssh-add -c -t 1h -h <host>`.
A set `ssh_agent_socket` now refuses the start and names its replacement rather than being
ignored. The kill switch empties that socket and logs ssh-add's own exit status.

Z7 -- zeo-terminals turned off for a running session. Its route was declared once; each call
now checks `agent.zeo_terminals` and the jail's Capabilities again, with the same policy the
declaration used (`v1::policy_for_launch`). No test: there is no harness for a routed call.

Z8 -- a secret's variable may not take a name the unjailed supervisor reads: `LD_*`,
`AI_JAIL*`, `SSL_*`, `*_PROXY` join the reserved names, case-insensitively.

Z9 -- `JailSpawn` no longer derives `Debug`: its env carries the real secret values; the
hand-written one prints names only.

Z10 -- the kill switch sent one SIGTERM and never checked. It now opens a pidfd before the
signal and, from a thread, SIGKILLs through it if the process has not exited within 2 s, so
the escalation can only reach the process it was opened for, never one that reused the pid.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
